From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: smolemaru <axyenniiparen@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 23:00:00 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] feat(providers): strengthen the keyless free route
 (Kilo + OpenCode Zen), and sink the dead upstreams

TachiDesk vendor patch #2 — applies ON TOP OF freellmapi-ovh-scaleway.patch.

This does not add a provider the user has to pick. It makes the ONE keyless
free route stronger: more upstreams behind the single entry point, ordered by
what actually answers, with the dead weight taken out of the failover path.

Probed live 2026-08-01 (nonce in every prompt, so a cached 200 could not pass
for a live answer). Every status code below was measured, not read from docs:

  ADDED
  - kilo  (api.kilo.ai/api/gateway) — 200 on every :free row with no usable
    credential; a garbage bearer also 200s, so the header is ignored outright.
    The platform was ALREADY registered upstream but carried one catalog row
    and was never seeded a key, so the router skipped it. 8 rows added,
    including a 1M-context nemotron, an omni/reasoning row and a coding row.
  - zen   (opencode.ai/zen/v1)      — 200 keyless on all 6 free rows. Needs
    `omitAuth`: a present-but-bogus bearer 401s. Ranked BELOW Kilo and treated
    as best-effort — OpenCode's docs say these need billing details on file,
    yet they answer without; that gap can close without warning.

  DISABLED (measured dead — a 4xx upstream is worse than an absent one,
  because it burns a failover attempt before the user gets an answer)
  - ovh, all 4 rows — 403 x5 "Forbidden: authentication failed … generate a
    new one at …/oauth/ovh/authorize". The anonymous tier V12 vendored this
    platform FOR is gone, replaced by OAuth. Not rate-limiting; dead.
  - pollinations openai-fast — 402 Payment Required x4 + a deprecation notice
    for the legacy text API, and it took 22-24s to return that 402 twice.
  - llm7, 4 of 5 rows — 400 "Model is currently unavailable" (stale ids).
    codestral-latest still answers 200 and is KEPT enabled.

  ORDERED
  New rows would otherwise land at MAX(priority)+n, i.e. dead last, burying
  the best upstream. Four rows get an explicit negative lead priority; the
  rest sit at the tail. The lead band is small on purpose — the router's
  penalty caps at MAX_PENALTY, so a lead row can sink but never fall past the
  keyed providers, making each one a round-trip the user pays for if that
  upstream dies. The 4th slot is a different vendor so a Kilo-wide outage
  fails over across gateways instead of deeper into the same one.

Disclosure that must not be lost: every Kilo :free row self-reports
`mayTrainOnYourPrompts: true`. Kilo is now reachable WITHOUT the user picking
it, so that fact is surfaced on the FreeLLM router's own surface in the host
app (registry hint, Free Providers card, provider picker).

ROUTING, added 2026-08-02 after a packaged driver run proved the free route was
broken even where this patch HAD landed. Two defects, both in server/src/routes/
proxy.ts, both measured on the shipped build:

  1. A REJECTED CREDENTIAL WAS A DEAD END, NOT A FAILOVER. `isRetryableError`
     matched 429/5xx/transport only, so a 401 fell to the `else` and became an
     immediate 502 carrying the upstream's words verbatim
     (`Provider error (Qwen3 Coder (free)): OpenRouter API error 401: User not
     found.`). Twelve upstreams sat behind that row and none were tried.
     Failover is the entire value of the router; a bad key is exactly the case
     it exists for.
     Fixed with a SEPARATE classifier, not by widening the retry one — the two
     need different treatment. A 429 sinks the MODEL for a while; a 401
     condemns the KEY. Retry alone would loop forever: the dynamic penalty caps
     at MAX_PENALTY (10), so a permanently-401ing row can never sink past the
     keyed providers and is re-picked on the next send. So the handler also
     writes `status='invalid'`, which routeRequest() already filters BEFORE it
     opens a socket. That is what makes the recovery permanent instead of
     per-request, and the dashboard still shows the row with an honest status
     so the user can see why a platform went quiet.

  2. NO MODEL COULD BE PINNED FROM THE HOST APP. The composer sends
     `${platform}/${modelId}` — it has to, because a bare id is ambiguous — and
     the relay only ever looked up bare ids, so every pinned relay model 400'd.
     Worse, `nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b:free` exists on BOTH `openrouter`
     and `kilo`, and the bare lookup returns whichever row was inserted first,
     which left Kilo's copy unaddressable by any string a client could send.
     Model resolution now tries BARE FIRST and only then splits on the FIRST
     slash. That order is load-bearing: `nvidia` and `cohere` are both vendor
     prefixes inside model ids AND platform names, so splitting first would
     misread every vendor-prefixed id. Splitting on the first slash only keeps
     the qualified form unambiguous — the platform set is closed, and everything
     after that slash is the model id verbatim, however many slashes it holds.

Touched: shared Platform union, OpenAICompatProvider (omitAuth), provider
registrations, keys-route allowlist, KeysPage labels, models migration V13,
proxy credential failover + platform-qualified model addressing, plus two
regression tests — one pinning the failover order, one pinning the collision
and the 401 failover.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
